    [38;5;153m0[39m/[38;5;153mfalse[39m/[38;5;153mno[39m → [38;5;153mFalse[39m, anything else → treated as a CA bundle path (requests' own [38;5;153mverify[39m contract).
  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m — sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m, so staging skips verification.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m — untouched; the nightly production export keeps full certificate verification.

  Verified the three env cases resolve to [38;5;153mTrue[39m / [38;5;153mFalse[39m / the bundle path. [38;5;153mrequests[39m will emit an [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m
  per call on the staging run; I left that unsuppressed so the insecure path stays visible. If you do have the staging
  CA cert on disk, pointing [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m at it instead of [38;5;153m0[39m gets staging working with verification intact — no
  code change needed.

